1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,851
This is ECE 320, video 10 b. We continue the pipeline diagram questions from past exams.

2
00:00:06,301 --> 00:00:13,500
After this video, you can find a forwarding optimization and its logic, as in fall 2019, question 2 part B.

3
00:00:13,950 --> 00:00:20,916
You can fill a diagram when the branch compare unit gets no forwarding. That is the fall 2022 paper, question 3.

4
00:00:21,366 --> 00:00:23,037
And you can check your cycle count.

5
00:00:23,487 --> 00:00:29,571
The table from video 10 a gives the path, or the number of stalls, for each distance between the producer and the consumer.

6
00:00:30,021 --> 00:00:42,188
Look at the third row: store data. Two instructions later, it costs 2 stalls, because store data has no WX path. The store reads x1 in D only after the W of add.

7
00:00:42,638 --> 00:00:53,830
The rule behind the table: a path needs both stages in the same cycle. If there is no path, the consumer stalls in D. After any stall, compare the real cycles again.

8
00:00:54,280 --> 00:01:01,014
Part B of the same question asks for an optimization of the forwarding hardware, and for the new forwarding logic.

9
00:01:01,464 --> 00:01:04,390
Forwarding into the decode stage is not allowed.

10
00:01:04,840 --> 00:01:13,199
The diagram is the Part A answer from video 10 a: 23 cycles. Pause the video and look for the stalls that a new path could remove.

11
00:01:13,649 --> 00:01:18,479
Number 6 store word stalls 2 cycles in D, in cycles 9 and 10.

12
00:01:18,929 --> 00:01:22,040
Its store data x6 comes from add number 4.

13
00:01:22,490 --> 00:01:27,922
In cycle 10, add number 4 is in W. So the new x6 already exists.

14
00:01:28,372 --> 00:01:33,620
Number 6 could be in X in cycle 10. The base address can already take WX.

15
00:01:34,070 --> 00:01:39,921
Only a path into the store-data input is missing. That missing path is the optimization.

16
00:01:40,371 --> 00:01:50,354
The sketch shows the class pipeline, as on slide 39. The store data, rs2 X, goes from the D X register straight to the X M register.

17
00:01:50,804 --> 00:01:56,981
Now the forwarding paths. MX and WX go into the two muxes in front of the ALU.

18
00:01:57,431 --> 00:02:01,146
WM goes into a mux at the data input of the data memory.

19
00:02:01,596 --> 00:02:06,054
The red line is the store data in X. It passes no forwarding mux.

20
00:02:06,504 --> 00:02:12,588
In cycle 10, add number 4 is in W. Its new x6 is in the M W register.

21
00:02:13,038 --> 00:02:20,965
Number 6 is in D and wants to go to X. No path can bring the new x6 to the store data of number 6.

22
00:02:21,415 --> 00:02:27,452
The optimization: add a mux on the store-data line in X, before the X M register.

23
00:02:27,902 --> 00:02:37,654
Its new input comes from the WX path: the value in the M W register. So the new x6 replaces the old x6 from rs2 X.

24
00:02:38,104 --> 00:02:44,467
The forwarding control logic drives the select input of the new mux. The next slide shows the logic.

25
00:02:44,917 --> 00:02:55,737
The new logic compares register numbers in the pipeline registers, like the existing forwarding logic. Condition 1: the instruction in X is a store. Number 6 is a store.

26
00:02:56,187 --> 00:03:04,175
Condition 2: its rs2 equals the destination register of the instruction in M W. x6 equals x6.

27
00:03:04,625 --> 00:03:10,894
Condition 3: that register is not x0. x6 is not x0.

28
00:03:11,344 --> 00:03:15,895
All three conditions are true, so the AND gives select equal to 1.

29
00:03:16,345 --> 00:03:23,033
With select 1, the mux passes the M W value, the new x6, to rs2 M.

30
00:03:23,483 --> 00:03:27,244
With select 0, it passes rs2 X, as before.

31
00:03:27,694 --> 00:03:37,725
The new logic has the same form as the logic on slide 39. There, if rs1 in D X equals rd in X M, the A mux selects the MX input.

32
00:03:38,175 --> 00:03:45,327
If rs1 in D X equals rd in M W, the A mux selects the WX input.

33
00:03:45,777 --> 00:03:54,244
So the answer for Part B: add a WX path into the store-data input, with the select logic above. It is a model answer, not an official one.

34
00:03:54,694 --> 00:04:00,870
The diagram now has the new path. In cycle 9, number 6 is in D, and nothing stops it now.

35
00:04:01,320 --> 00:04:09,819
In cycle 10, add number 4 is in W. WM gives x6 to the store data of number 5, as before.

36
00:04:10,269 --> 00:04:15,331
The new WX path gives x6 to number 6, for its base and its store data.

37
00:04:15,781 --> 00:04:27,902
The run now ends in cycle 21. Part A ended in cycle 23. 23 minus 21 is 2 cycles: the 2 D star cycles of number 6 are gone.

38
00:04:28,352 --> 00:04:34,327
Three stalls are left. Count the D star cells: number 3, number 4 and number 14.

39
00:04:34,777 --> 00:04:42,254
Number 3 and number 14 have load-use stalls. No path can help them, because a load value exists only at the end of M.

40
00:04:42,704 --> 00:04:49,112
Number 4 has the W D case. Only forwarding into D could remove it, and the question excludes that.

41
00:04:49,562 --> 00:04:57,132
The next question is from the fall 2022 paper, question 3, for 16 marks. The file name is f 2020 midterm.

42
00:04:57,582 --> 00:05:00,647
There is forwarding as in class, but none into D.

43
00:05:01,097 --> 00:05:10,153
One rule is new. There is no forwarding into the branch compare unit. So the branch must read both operands in D, after the W of each producer.

44
00:05:10,603 --> 00:05:15,758
Branches resolve in X, and the pipeline guesses not taken until the branch resolves.

45
00:05:16,208 --> 00:05:18,559
The branch in instruction 4 is taken.

46
00:05:19,009 --> 00:05:24,163
A flushed stage gets a dash. Then you fill the forwarding table and the stall table.

47
00:05:24,613 --> 00:05:34,459
The paper writes L W x5, x4 of x1, in number 5 and number 7. That is not valid RISC-V. Read it as an offset of 4.

48
00:05:34,909 --> 00:05:46,101
If you read it as x4 plus x1, the timing does not change, because x4 was written in cycle 5, long before. Write your assumption in the exam. Pause the video and try the question.

49
00:05:46,551 --> 00:05:56,442
Step 1: the producers. Number 1 load word reads x2, and nothing older writes x2. Number 1 writes x4.

50
00:05:56,892 --> 00:06:02,512
Number 2 store word uses x4 as store data. The producer is the load number 1.

51
00:06:02,962 --> 00:06:09,185
Number 3 add reads x4 two times. The producer is also number 1.

52
00:06:09,635 --> 00:06:16,342
Number 4 B E Q compares x4 and x1. x4 comes from the load number 1.

53
00:06:16,792 --> 00:06:18,788
x1 comes from add number 3.

54
00:06:19,238 --> 00:06:20,260
The branch is taken.

55
00:06:20,710 --> 00:06:25,958
So number 5 and number 6 are on the wrong path. They are flushed, and they are not producers.

56
00:06:26,408 --> 00:06:36,113
Number 7 reads x1. Number 6 also writes x1, but number 6 is flushed. So number 7 gets x1 from add number 3.

57
00:06:36,563 --> 00:06:44,551
Number 7 load word writes x5. Number 8 uses x5 as base and as data. The producer is number 7.

58
00:06:45,001 --> 00:06:49,506
Number 9 and number 10 also read x5 from number 7.

59
00:06:49,956 --> 00:06:56,736
Number 11 reads only x0. Number 12 reads x8, and nothing writes x8.

60
00:06:57,186 --> 00:07:06,102
Now the diagram. In cycles 1 to 4, one instruction enters per cycle: the load, the store, the add and the B E Q.

61
00:07:06,552 --> 00:07:20,330
In cycle 5, the load is in W. Number 2 is in M, and it needs x4 as store data. So x4 goes by WM. There is no load-use stall, because store data is needed only in M.

62
00:07:20,780 --> 00:07:28,582
Number 3 is in X, and it needs x4. So x4 goes by WX. One W feeds two forwards.

63
00:07:29,032 --> 00:07:34,790
Number 4 B E Q is in D in cycle 5, and it waits. The next steps show why.

64
00:07:35,240 --> 00:07:42,890
Number 4 B E Q gets no forwarded values. It must read x4 and x1 in D after their W.

65
00:07:43,340 --> 00:07:46,451
x4 is written by the load number 1 in cycle 5.

66
00:07:46,901 --> 00:07:51,174
x1 is written by add number 3 in W, in cycle 7.

67
00:07:51,624 --> 00:07:55,803
So B E Q repeats D in cycles 5, 6 and 7.

68
00:07:56,253 --> 00:07:58,876
Number 5 repeats F in the same cycles.

69
00:07:59,326 --> 00:08:03,877
In cycle 8, B E Q reads x4 and x1 from the register file.

70
00:08:04,327 --> 00:08:07,439
Number 5 does its last F in cycle 8.

71
00:08:07,889 --> 00:08:12,440
In cycle 9, B E Q is in X. It resolves: taken.

72
00:08:12,890 --> 00:08:20,227
The pipeline guessed not taken. So number 5, now in D, and number 6, now in F, are on the wrong path.

73
00:08:20,677 --> 00:08:24,903
In cycle 10, number 5 and number 6 are flushed. They get dashes.

74
00:08:25,353 --> 00:08:28,511
T1, instruction 7, is fetched in cycle 10.

75
00:08:28,961 --> 00:08:44,519
Number 7 needs x1. Number 6 also writes x1, but number 6 is flushed. So the producer is add number 3. It wrote x1 in cycle 7. Number 7 reads x1 in D in cycle 11. No stall.

76
00:08:44,969 --> 00:08:58,622
Number 8 store word uses x5 as base and as data. The producer is the load number 7. In cycle 12, number 8 is in D. Its X would be cycle 13, while the load is in M.

77
00:08:59,072 --> 00:09:05,249
The base is needed in X. This is load-use, so number 8 stalls in cycle 12.

78
00:09:05,699 --> 00:09:14,383
In cycle 13, number 8 tries again. Its X would be cycle 14, while the load is in W. The base could take WX.

79
00:09:14,833 --> 00:09:21,195
But the store data has no WX path in the class datapath. So number 8 stalls again in cycle 13.

80
00:09:21,645 --> 00:09:33,933
In cycle 14, the load writes x5 in W. Number 8 is in D in the same cycle, so it reads the old x5. This is the W D case, and D repeats once more.

81
00:09:34,383 --> 00:09:38,655
Number 9 waits in F in cycles 12, 13 and 14.

82
00:09:39,105 --> 00:09:43,145
In cycle 15, number 8 reads x5 from the register file.

83
00:09:43,595 --> 00:09:53,812
Count the D star cells of number 8: 3 stall cycles. Each has a different reason: load-use, no WX path for store data, and the W D case.

84
00:09:54,262 --> 00:10:04,154
Number 9 store word needs x5 as store data. The load wrote x5 in cycle 14. Number 9 reads it in D in cycle 16. No stall.

85
00:10:04,604 --> 00:10:09,248
Number 10 reads x5 from the register file in cycle 17. No stall.

86
00:10:09,698 --> 00:10:24,466
Number 11 and number 12 do not read x5. They only write it. The writes of number 10, number 11 and number 12 come in program order, in cycles 20, 21 and 22. So these write after write pairs are safe.

87
00:10:24,916 --> 00:10:27,005
The run ends in cycle 22.

88
00:10:27,455 --> 00:10:40,180
Now the two tables. There are only two forwards, both from the load number 1 in cycle 5. Row 1: x4 goes from W to M, into the store data of number 2.

89
00:10:40,630 --> 00:10:44,902
Row 2: x4 goes from W to X, into number 3.

90
00:10:45,352 --> 00:10:52,551
The stall table. Number 4 stalls in D in cycles 5, 6 and 7, because the compare unit gets no forwarding.

91
00:10:53,001 --> 00:11:00,710
Number 5 stalls in F in the same cycles. Number 5 is flushed later, but its F stars are real stalls. Write them in the table.

92
00:11:01,160 --> 00:11:13,466
Number 8 stalls in D in cycles 12, 13 and 14. Write the three reasons: load-use for the base, no WX path for the store data, and the W D case.

93
00:11:13,916 --> 00:11:16,378
Number 9 stalls in F in the same cycles.

94
00:11:16,828 --> 00:11:24,444
One more check, because it shows the rules well. On the left is our answer, with the class datapath. Number 8 stalls 3 cycles.

95
00:11:24,894 --> 00:11:34,275
On the right, we add the WX path into store data from fall 2019 part B. Now number 8 stalls only 1 cycle, for load-use.

96
00:11:34,725 --> 00:11:40,251
In cycle 14, number 8 gets x5 by WX, for the base and the data.

97
00:11:40,701 --> 00:11:50,964
But now number 9 moves one cycle earlier. Its D is in cycle 14, the cycle of the load's W. That is the W D case, so number 9 stalls once.

98
00:11:51,414 --> 00:12:00,006
The run takes 21 cycles. The new path is not part of the exam answer. The check shows that you must apply every rule again after a change.

99
00:12:00,456 --> 00:12:04,728
The check for your cycle count is not on the slides. The count has four parts.

100
00:12:05,178 --> 00:12:12,469
One instruction alone takes 5 cycles. Its W comes 4 cycles after its F, so the count starts with 4.

101
00:12:12,919 --> 00:12:18,724
Each instruction that finishes adds one cycle: its W comes one cycle after the W before it.

102
00:12:19,174 --> 00:12:22,796
Each D star delays all later instructions by one cycle.

103
00:12:23,246 --> 00:12:27,054
Each taken branch adds 2 cycles, for the 2 flushed instructions.

104
00:12:27,504 --> 00:12:35,167
Assignment 3 part a: 6 instructions and 12 D star cycles. 4 plus 6 plus 12 is 22.

105
00:12:35,617 --> 00:12:41,422
Part b: 6 instructions and 1 D star cycle. 4 plus 6 plus 1 is 11.

106
00:12:41,872 --> 00:12:56,593
Fall 2019 Part A: 14 instructions, but 2 are flushed, so 12 finish. There are 5 D star cycles and 1 taken branch. 4 plus 12 plus 5 plus 2 is 23.

107
00:12:57,043 --> 00:13:04,845
Fall 2019 Part B: only 3 D star cycles are left. 4 plus 12 plus 3 plus 2 is 21.

108
00:13:05,295 --> 00:13:17,323
Fall 2022: 12 instructions, 2 flushed, so 10 finish. There are 6 D star cycles and 1 taken branch. 4 plus 10 plus 6 plus 2 is 22.

109
00:13:17,773 --> 00:13:24,461
Do not count the F stars. They happen in the same cycles as the D stars, so they add no time.

110
00:13:24,911 --> 00:13:30,205
If your diagram and the count disagree, look for a missing stall or a missing flush.

111
00:13:30,655 --> 00:13:39,850
The recap of video 10 b. One. For an optimization question, find a stall whose value already sits in a pipeline register, and add the missing path.

112
00:13:40,300 --> 00:13:50,052
Two. In fall 2019 part B, the missing path is WX into the store-data input. The run goes from 23 to 21 cycles.

113
00:13:50,502 --> 00:14:01,303
Three. Its logic: the instruction in X is a store, and its rs2 equals rd in M W. Also, rd is not x0. Then select the M W value.

114
00:14:01,753 --> 00:14:09,599
Four. With no forwarding into the branch compare unit, the branch reads its operands in D. That D comes after the W of every producer.

115
00:14:10,049 --> 00:14:16,179
Five. A store whose base and data come from the load right before it stalls 3 cycles in the class datapath.

116
00:14:16,629 --> 00:14:22,593
Six. Flushed rows are never producers, but their F stars still go in the stall table.

117
00:14:23,043 --> 00:14:33,771
Seven. Check your count: 4, plus the finished instructions, plus the D stars, plus 2 per taken branch. That is the end of video 10.
